field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-336093/field-3/889e90ae731e07f997a6fb79ba79ebf9ad84d732e2e74ecb55bc62928487747c" :answer-field/choices ["q-336093/field-3/ed638285b2b39b62ade902fa60b790fb68a87a79b2b1ac91eb5ea9897f39ed8f" "q-336093/field-3/3ed4572870e4d0baf5a9cbebe5ea2fb78ded703323eddc77292eb22b6e00178a" "q-336093/field-3/c9fcc813d07eaabb5860e77a9cdd1ff7f681d7c81093ed4851ee4d3510ffde85" "q-336093/field-3/889e90ae731e07f997a6fb79ba79ebf9ad84d732e2e74ecb55bc62928487747c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336093" :question/id #uuid "b44d1fb2-d7c8-55d2-b679-198d5cddb501" :question/math-academy-id "q-336093" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ states the independence of the columns of {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L3}$ by the {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L2}.$ Line $\\text{L2}$ states that the two columns of $Φ(t)$ are linearly independent, which is required of a fundamental matrix. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ states independence of the columns of $Φ(t).$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the $\\text{properties of invertible matrices}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-336093/field-1" "q-336093/field-2" "q-336093/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0aea7503-74d6-5ba4-9411-ee53e59512d0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336093"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333719/field-1/38344b31510a467d41493f335ca5876e93d55a68d152eb925102abdda5b710ad" :answer/id #uuid "7eab1923-2276-560f-93be-45a76a3d41ba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{1}$ is a solution of the system" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333719/field-1/1c61c573cf7ae6cac5f1f6aacce1afbc39bb4525f01739c00ce9089c715b3655" :answer/id #uuid "d8c7a74c-c437-5341-a2f7-7a76a160e973" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333719/field-1/84843dd7c89252741db4853ef9bdccc76acae5f62f639b02f027c3d432519fdd" :answer/id #uuid "b3f23bc2-d64a-5951-9e0f-d9f449aa772a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${x}_{1}$ is differentiable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333719/field-1/c8a64c4b4dd31a8f49b1a6d0f62ddcbc9421b30c69b2ba7584fbb56ce1a35d48" :answer/id #uuid "11ba6736-b692-580d-add0-cd3c868fdab4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333719/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4201ccef-34e9-5f48-b942-ab59780d5285" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333719/field-1/38344b31510a467d41493f335ca5876e93d55a