9/field-2/314a8bf3988f29de8fd66f4fbfe199e21705e2d339ca177314f13a77b18830f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333719/field-3/1312b864e8a71b0fb8c8ff97e69337e9448f828f4c635fa3893fb62df18554ab" :answer/id #uuid "fd66c7f4-34b7-5938-afed-adb06f4a1cf6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "factoring out $A$ on the left" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333719/field-3/ec4853b3921019d5244ff4728b011f929b731719e7770407ed693533d55267ad" :answer/id #uuid "b3097db5-bba2-5148-ae25-8f8408a353c7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "adding $Φ(t)$ to both sides" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333719/field-3/d5668059631d1826dc438c5e08df911b7db22d677a287df55f89894e9cd4645f" :answer/id #uuid "5e299e12-e1dc-58c6-9374-f197ae95e3ef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "transposing the matrix" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333719/field-3/eb09a9b372ce1dd8f273bfb534a23ab2315fb3889256901de6da6220ed393e4f" :answer/id #uuid "ff8139f8-7179-57e4-b4c9-9ae6844b98db" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "differentiating again" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333719/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "28c4b610-d9eb-54e5-b7fc-fdb2720eff96" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333719/field-3/1312b864e8a71b0fb8c8ff97e69337e9448f828f4c635fa3893fb62df18554ab" :answer-field/choices ["q-333719/field-3/1312b864e8a71b0fb8c8ff97e69337e9448f828f4c635fa3893fb62df18554ab" "q-333719/field-3/ec4853b3921019d5244ff4728b011f929b731719e7770407ed693533d55267ad" "q-333719/field-3/d5668059631d1826dc438c5e08df911b7db22d677a287df55f89894e9cd4645f" "q-333719/field-3/eb09a9b372ce1dd8f273bfb534a23ab2315fb3889256901de6da6220ed393e4f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333719" :question/id #uuid "49e5968a-1ea4-5510-81b8-9a383e60e6d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-333719" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A\\,Φ(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $[a,\\,b]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $a$ and $b.$*\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[{x}_{1}^{′}(t),\\,{x}_{2}^{′}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[A{x}_{1}(t),\\,A{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=AΦ(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L4}$ and substitution from lines {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ This means that each column satisfies the differential equation. In particular, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L2}$ follows from the fact that ${x}_{1}\\text{ is a solution of the system}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L4}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ By definition, the derivative of $Φ(t)$ is obtained by differentiating each column,\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}^{′}(t) & {x}_{2}^{′}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$ from $\\text{L3},$ we substitute each derivative into the matrix to get\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by substitution from $\\text{L2}\\text{ and }\\text{L3}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}]=A[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}]=AΦ(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{factoring out }A\\text{ on the left}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-333719/field-1" "q-333719/field-2" "q-333719/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0aea7503-74d6-5ba4-9411-ee53e59512d0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333719"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220537 :answer-field/correct 17592186220538 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220527 :answer-field/correct 17592186220529 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220532 :answer-field/correct 17592186220535 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-333723"] :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that every solution $x(t)$ can be written in the form $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c=[{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}]^{T}$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of solutions\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)$ can be written as $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)\\,c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Once ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis, every solution $x(t)$ of the system can be expressed uniquely as a linear combination\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the definition of a basis}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are the columns of $Φ(t),$ we can write\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen by the definition of matrix multiplication,\n\n$$\nΦ(t)\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of matrix multiplication}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-334382/field-1/82388720364d9f7b2e275b435e63a35917c46caa3e0c1390ecd26ba25cbd7b07" :answer/id #uuid "fee90bc1-c28c-5ef4-b0bd-63c5765116e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\cos(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334382/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8b0ac857-5327-5216-889b-13ad93622cdb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334382/field-1/82388720364d9f7b2e275b435e63a35917c46caa3e0c1390ecd26ba25cbd7b07" :answer-field/choices ["q-334382/field-1/82388720364d9f7b2e275b435e63a35917c46caa3e0c1390ecd26ba25cbd7b07"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334382/field-2/3acf776ab4193c87de4c38f6bf67c50f332a58c7119405db24978be314f88878" :answer/id #uuid "f4c5bdcf-6cc5-55b0-87eb-4eb3b015f4db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sin(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334382/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "d2eafe6d-de82-5312-933e-800295acf934" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334382/field-2/3acf776ab4193c87de4c38f6bf67c50f332a58c7119405db24978be314f88878" :answer-field/choices ["q-334382/field-2/3acf776ab4193c87de4c38f6bf67c50f332a58c7119405db24978be314f88878"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334382/field-3/1120053172f0773852e431e0cfddb1fb8f0cf0ffb32e083fc4e8ae70bb3f9abc" :answer/id #uuid "25867c2a-6e3d-5246-9ab3-39cb41e028e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sin(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334382/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "23b741fd-2f68-5d99-8191-bf8bca142f31" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334382/field-3/1120053172f0773852e431e0cfddb1fb8f0cf0ffb32e083fc4e8ae70bb3f9abc" :answer-field/choices ["q-334382/field-3/1120053172f0773852e431e0cfddb1fb8f0cf0ffb32e083fc4e8ae70bb3f9abc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334382/field-4/82388720364d9f7b2e275b435e63a35917c46caa3e0c1390ecd26ba25cbd7b07" :answer/id #uuid "ec7572be-44ad-5bfb-8f15-da9f25b854bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\cos(5t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334382/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "edab2a91-901c-5d06-9847-de5573b5f0df" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334382/field-4/82388720364d9f7b2e275b435e63a35917c46caa3e0c1390ecd26ba25cbd7b07" :answer-field/choices ["q-334382/field-4/82388720364d9f7b2e275b435e63a35917c46caa3e0c1390ecd26ba25cbd7b07"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334382" :question/id #uuid "0fceb88b-91dc-5f03-80e2-15ef97ea1801" :question/math-academy-id "q-334382" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-5\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((5\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(5\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(5\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+5\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-334382/field-1" "q-334382/field-2" "q-334382/field-3" "q-334382/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c99cda-25a5-50f2-903b-bfb1fdc5fa10"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334382"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333715/field-1/ba4912de24ffee9c19c27e46152d580b54519f36db4d14f6ce25ea1236e2c9c0" :answer/id #uuid "3bdf8f41-2462-5d25-bbbd-b7a4a1236b25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{2t}-e^{-2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333715/field-1/ce5202d1220da7b9893b00224840c4b3387d0946e233ae159f92cc06aa4a0137" :answer/id #uuid "0cf80a27-ea35-55e5-a849-3526c74326f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{2t}+e^{-2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333715/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d18f5c5a-b527-5237-82b7-e1387ef8edbe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333715/field-1/ba4912de24ffee9c19c27e46152d580b54519f36db4d14f6ce25ea1236e2c9c0" :answer-field/choices ["q-333715/field-1/ba4912de24ffee9c19c27e46152d580b54519f36db4d14f6ce25ea1236e2c9c0" "q-333715/field-1/ce5202d1220da7b9893b00224840c4b3387d0946e233ae159f92cc06aa4a0137"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333715/field-2/ba4912de24ffee9c19c27e46152d580b54519f36db4d14f6ce25ea1236e2c9c0" :answer/id #uuid "78b6da1e-79b9-56a5-957e-b812e2961d2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{2t}-e^{-2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333715/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4f4d921c-9517-56fd-80f2-5155fc111c84" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-333715/field-2/ba4912de24ffee9c19c27e46152d580b54519f36db4d14f6ce25ea1236e2c9c0" :answer-field/choices ["q-333715/field-2/ba4912de24ffee9c19c27e46152d580b54519f36db4d14f6ce25ea1236e2c9c0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333715" :question/id #uuid "5b85c03f-1b1b-545b-8035-6251ef2d11b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333715" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$ |  | ${e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | ${e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-2t)={e}^{-2t} \\\\ r(2t)={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtraction the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{4t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-333715/field-1" "q-333715/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c99cda-25a5-50f2-903b-bfb1fdc5fa10"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333715"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334499/field-1/4549aae17f8c8103684e051f55b2d30deee83a112b49d939d0eea2f5c809b2be" :answer/id #uuid "d9230be7-2c4c-5461-a20e-e69c68217034" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{5t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334499/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "96e95ae5-da18-5b06-a8b0-99542b8752ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334499/field-1/4549aae17f8c8103684e051f55b2d30deee83a112b49d939d0eea2f5c809b2be" :answer-field/choices ["q-334499/field-1/4549aae17f8c8103684e051f55b2d30deee83a112b49d939d0eea2f5c809b2be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334499/field-2/bc2480761f22ff45e4c6b6e089112b71d285ae620b456c26b2d54aac0a82f67e" :answer/id #uuid "9202e141-66d7-510a-9a10-538655f1700a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "te^{5t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334499/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "758c0327-04db-5677-8fc3-e534e219ed09" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334499/field-2/bc2480761f22ff45e4c6b6e089112b71d285ae620b456c26b2d54aac0a82f67e" :answer-field/choices ["q-334499/field-2/bc2480761f22ff45e4c6b6e089112b71d285ae620b456c26b2d54aac0a82f67e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334499/field-3/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "82f9c4e3-5a68-5858-9583-18c21a3e1b23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334499/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "3fbda469-2ba9-553b-b3e3-713eb9db8faa" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334499/field-3/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-334499/field-3/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334499/field-4/4549aae17f8c8103684e051f55b2d30deee83a112b49d939d0eea2f5c809b2be" :answer/id #uuid "8b920bda-1dd4-5194-abf2-7a9da42619a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{5t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334499/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "558d8559-ed04-5b67-9d2c-6f4c73a070b5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334499/field-4/4549aae17f8c8103684e051f55b2d30deee83a112b49d939d0eea2f5c809b2be" :answer-field/choices ["q-334499/field-4/4549aae17f8c8103684e051f55b2d30deee83a112b49d939d0eea2f5c809b2be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334499" :question/id #uuid "9f6832ec-b78d-5fbd-8a7b-80d82c69a785" :question/math-academy-id "q-334499" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(5t)={e}^{5t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(5t)={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}5t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{5t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n5t{e}^{5t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t}(1-5t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5{e}^{5t}t+{e}^{5t}(1-5t) \\\\  & ={e}^{5t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & ={e}^{5t}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334499/field-1" "q-334499/field-2" "q-334499/field-3" "q-334499/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c99cda-25a5-50f2-903b-bfb1fdc5fa10"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334499"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334387/field-1/3b45c8aace279ed882566c4cf35db160a841f81035275869b34657a3aa43d057" :answer/id #uuid "b46abe0b-0664-5ef5-a2b2-736b760f0161" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{3t}+e^{-3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334387/field-1/82c64b091a694b4c3228116916a9908a5a8d108b7f0b1186e74bf76a509e4124" :answer/id #uuid "8ead1a0f-5067-5807-acf3-573fa8e124e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{3t}-e^{-3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334387/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0e02ad73-6b78-5fa3-a6a3-27e8037536f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334387/field-1/3b45c8aace279ed882566c4cf35db160a841f81035275869b34657a3aa43d057" :answer-field/choices ["q-334387/field-1/3b45c8aace279ed882566c4cf35db160a841f81035275869b34657a3aa43d057" "q-334387/field-1/82c64b091a694b4c3228116916a9908a5a8d108b7f0b1186e74bf76a509e4124"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334387/field-2/3b45c8aace279ed882566c4cf35db160a841f81035275869b34657a3aa43d057" :answer/id #uuid "aada60eb-b96a-5863-9188-d39799ac65f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{3t}+e^{-3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334387/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6657f3b-0ad3-5e9f-86fd-284c93bbcf78" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334387/field-2/3b45c8aace279ed882566c4cf35db160a841f81035275869b34657a3aa43d057" :answer-field/choices ["q-334387/field-2/3b45c8aace279ed882566c4cf35db160a841f81035275869b34657a3aa43d057"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334387" :question/id #uuid "1995d3c4-602c-5f0c-b18d-81930b27c672" :question/math-academy-id "q-334387" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | ${e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | ${e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t}$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-3t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-3t)={e}^{-3t} \\\\ r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-3t} \\\\ 3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-6t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6t}({e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6t}({e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-334387/field-1" "q-334387/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c99cda-25a5-50f2-903b-bfb1fdc5fa10"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334387"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333713/field-1/2b8bb20e354d2eeeea362bae12c9ada61bfd29d22f12ed7588dfb1506405c2ee" :answer/id #uuid "646a2fc2-6b5d-5a56-a724-4e4a827fd36d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\cos(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220509 :answer-field/correct "q-333713/field-1/2b8bb20e354d2eeeea362bae12c9ada61bfd29d22f12ed7588dfb1506405c2ee" :answer-field/choices ["q-333713/field-1/2b8bb20e354d2eeeea362bae12c9ada61bfd29d22f12ed7588dfb1506405c2ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333713/field-2/5571d4126de2dfbf68393769a6e9108689104eac3f78c7c787d3111ad4a046e1" :answer/id #uuid "55eb7a7f-8a15-5ab1-a849-4d5c4c319311" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sin(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220508 :answer-field/correct "q-333713/field-2/5571d4126de2dfbf68393769a6e9108689104eac3f78c7c787d3111ad4a046e1" :answer-field/choices ["q-333713/field-2/5571d4126de2dfbf68393769a6e9108689104eac3f78c7c787d3111ad4a046e1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333713/field-3/82aac27628d304b42cd67c3e21fe9cea98c87072a3feaec8387f4eda1ccfa246" :answer/id #uuid "3a8c887e-fef9-5eec-b538-36b569af5acf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sin(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220510 :answer-field/correct "q-333713/field-3/82aac27628d304b42cd67c3e21fe9cea98c87072a3feaec8387f4eda1ccfa246" :answer-field/choices ["q-333713/field-3/82aac27628d304b42cd67c3e21fe9cea98c87072a3feaec8387f4eda1ccfa246"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333713/field-4/2b8bb20e354d2eeeea362bae12c9ada61bfd29d22f12ed7588dfb1506405c2ee" :answer/id #uuid "ddaee053-ecdb-5161-a21a-4c9140b274be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\cos(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220507 :answer-field/correct "q-333713/field-4/2b8bb20e354d2eeeea362bae12c9ada61bfd29d22f12ed7588dfb1506405c2ee" :answer-field/choices ["q-333713/field-4/2b8bb20e354d2eeeea362bae12c9ada61bfd29d22f12ed7588dfb1506405c2ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-333713"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+2\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21695"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/d9bdce761076544aff166409cc78bdf918c4afc2c47b67e6366e3aabb78087b7.png)\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2,$ shown above. Find the fundamental matrix for the system." :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system $\\mathbf{x}' = A \\mathbf{x},$ where $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $\\Phi(t)$ of the system is an $n \\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our cases, we have a $2 \\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}=[\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}=[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 4{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/bb710709a593f8d185cde601fb5082d7f79ed21af824c28949477d2011479d95.png)\n\n**Note:** The columns of $\\Phi$ can be written in any order." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21697"] :question/problem "Consider the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2 \\times 2$ matrix. Let $\\Phi(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $\\Phi(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation $\\Phi'(t) = A \\, \\Phi(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $\\big[ \\mathbf{a}, \\: \\mathbf{b} \\big]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}.$*\n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\Phi(t) = \\big[ \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_1'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_1(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_2'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = \\big[ \\mathbf{x}_1'(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2'(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) =  \\big[ A \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: A \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = A \\big[ \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = A \\Phi(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from the fact that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/be39ec10432eb81c3e00fdf30599353450e1ae7ebebd136f20dce45deb685cac.png).\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L5}$ by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/09febb48bb00d22caebf088103181b5db6377befccad18f1370718351cc91e9d.png).\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L7}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L6}$ and substitution from line ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/999db06dcfe0878ddb3316b11546668ba9442d8fd110165e5076b90964b84905.png)." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L2}.$ Since $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t).$ This means that each column satisfies the differential equation. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_1(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from the fact that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ is a solution of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L5}$ and $\\textrm{L6}.$ We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n\n$$\n\\Phi'(t)=\\big[A\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:A\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big]\n= A\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}$ by factoring out $A$ on the left.\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L6}$ and $\\textrm{L7}.$ From $\\textrm{L6}$ we have $\\Phi'(t)=A\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big].$ Using $\\Phi(t)=\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big]$ from $\\textrm{L1},$ we substitute to obtain $\\Phi'(t)=A\\Phi(t).$ Thus, $\\textrm{L7}$ follows from $\\textrm{L6}$ by substitution from $L1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21696"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/819e3b9672e898801ef7320c1d80246d218ae89e76b3247c86b642a5f5790400.png)\nConsider the system of linear ODEs $\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x}$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix $\\Phi(t)$ for this system." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\mathbf x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system $\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t),$ denoted $\\Phi_0(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n\\Phi_0'(t) = A\\Phi_0(t), \\quad \\Phi_0(0)  = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that $\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & -7{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 7\\textrm{i}t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2 = -7\\textrm{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((7\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(7\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(7\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+7\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{7t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{7t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070844/assets/4d6fd69672291bf4c39d33a04656258b89a500bc039588a9fd27eb10db328cc3.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
